@startuml timing-notes-shifted-grids
'' PlantUML over Mermaid because the shifted-grid argument is about intervals on ONE axis: a timing diagram's concise
'' lanes draw each grid as a row of consecutive cells over a shared horizontal axis, so a set of matches (a highlighted
'' span) can be read against the cell boundaries of both grids at once. Mermaid has no timing diagram with shared
'' highlights, no LaTeX in labels, and does not render byte-reproducibly.
'' Fills: orange = the pure notes model's objects (the cells, at the container tier; the copy that one grid finds, at the
'' component tier); red = the set that every grid splits. The axis is ln sigma, t_x or t_y (theory/12 §3.2); one unit
'' of the drawing is an eighth of a cell, so a cell is w = 8 units wide and the two grids are shifted by w/2 = 4.
!include _vv-theme.iuml
hide time-axis
<style>
timingDiagram {
  LineColor #4A4A4A
  LineThickness 1.2
}
</style>

title Two grids half a cell apart, along one axis: cells <latex>w</latex> wide, shifted by <latex>w/2</latex> — theory/12 §3.4

concise "a set of matches" as M
concise "grid, shift 0" as G0
concise "grid, shifted half a cell" as G1

@0
M is {-}
G0 is "cell 0" IR_FILL
G1 is "cell −1" IR_FILL
@4
G1 is "cell 0" IR_FILL
@6
M is "A" IR_TINT
@8
G0 is "cell 1" IR_FILL
@9
M is {-}
@12
G1 is "cell 1" IR_FILL
@16
G0 is "cell 2" IR_FILL
@19
M is "B" ERROR_TINT
@20
G1 is "cell 2" IR_FILL
@24
G0 is "cell 3" IR_FILL
@25
M is {-}
@28
G1 is "cell 3" IR_FILL
@32
G0 is {-}
G1 is {-}

highlight 6 to 9 IR_FILL : A
highlight 19 to 25 ERROR_FILL : B

legend bottom
  |= Colour |= Meaning |
  |<#E67E22>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;| a grid's cells, and the set <latex>A</latex>: its spread, <latex>3w/8 < w/2</latex>, holds at most one boundary of the two grids, so the other grid (here the shifted one) holds it in one cell |
  |<#E0211D>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;| the set <latex>B</latex>: its spread, <latex>w/2 < 3w/4 < w</latex>, holds a boundary of each grid, so every grid splits it |
  Soundness: two matches in one cell differ by less than <latex>w</latex>. Completeness: a spread below <latex>w/2</latex> on every axis
  lies in one cell of some grid. In three axes, <latex>(\ln\sigma, t_x, t_y)</latex>, the eight grids shift each axis by <latex>0</latex> or <latex>w/2</latex> on its own,
  and the grid that takes the right shift on every axis holds the set in one cell.
endlegend
@enduml
